  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 PM EDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nArea of weak isentropic lift continues advance across the area,\nwith light returns (primarily sprinkles or T amounts of precip)\nextending east to the I-69 corridor. Steadier rain has remained\nin NW parts of the area so far. Models have been showing a\nweakening trend over the past several runs, but given current\nand upstream radar returns this seems somewhat unrealistic for \nat least the next several hours. Pops have been continued into \nearly evening with a slow taper from southwest to northeast into\nthe overnight hours. Signs still seem to point towards a small \nlull in precip late tonight through at least Thursday morning. \n\nA closed upper low, currently over the Four Corners area, will fight \nto work east and eventually northeast along the stubborn SE US \nridge. A weaker northern stream trough will dive in as well. The \nsouthern stream wave will fill and weaken with NE extent as it is \nabsorbed into the northern stream trough. PWATs will steadily \nincrease through the day Thursday, peaking in the 1.75\" to locally \n2\" range during the afternoon and evening hours ahead of an eastward \nprogressing front. Models indicate some weak surface cyclogenesis \nthat rides along the front mainly Thursday night. All these factors \nshould lead to the best chances for measurable rainfall with average \namounts of a half to one inch. A low chance exists for thunderstorms \nembedded within the area that could aid in locally higher rainfall \namounts, but at this point expected rainfall should not cause \nsignificant impacts to water levels across the area. \n\nDrier air quickly works in from the north Friday as the overall \nupper flow becomes semi-zonal and deeper moisture is pushed well \nsouth. Temperatures this weekend into next week will settle back into \nthe 60s for highs and lows in the 40s with a hint of some upper 30s \npossibly coming for Tuesday morning.",
